WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.480
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:08.940
Jonathan Haidt, who is an author, social psychologist
and professor at NYU's Stern School of Business.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.060
He recently wrote an article for The Atlantic
called Why the Past ten Years of American

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.260
Life Have Been Uniquely Stupid.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.920
Jonathan, always great to talk to you.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.110
Speaker 5: Thanks, David.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:19.110
It's great to be back with you.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:25.060
Speaker 1: So in the in the article, you talk
about a number of different things.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:31.120
You lead with the story of Babel as a sort
of metaphor for what's happened in the United

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.129
States since the 20th, starting in the 20
tens and the sort of fractured nature of the

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:38.129
country.

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:40.850
You talk about social media, you talk about
political discourse.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.889
You've talked about the impact of Trumpism,
sort of on discourse and society for people

00:00:46.889 --> 00:00:51.519
who maybe have not yet read the article and
will link to it, and everybody should.

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:57.320
Can you give us sort of a flowchart, directional
flowchart of the phenomena you observe?

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.000
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Yes.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Thank you for that for that.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.980
That's a good setup because we think in narrative,
we think in stories, things make sense.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.659
What we're going through now is so hard to
understand, but if we tell the story, it goes

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:10.950
like this.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.530
In 1989, the Berlin Wall Falls.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.310
We have this incredible period of optimism
about democracy.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.020
This is also the period of the early Internet.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.270
We had this incredible period of optimism
about technology for those who remember the

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.270
early Earth.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.259
And it was this magical, incredible place.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:29.329
We get into the early 2000.

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:30.590
Of course, we do have 911.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.890
We do have things get a little darker.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:39.179
But that decade, the early social media platforms,
the optimism, Wikipedia, I mean, it was just

00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:43.259
incredible what this technology was going
to do for democracy.

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:47.039
I'd like to bring readers back now to the
peak year of 2011, a year that starts with

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:48.689
the Arab Spring.

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:54.220
And we think what dictator can stand up to
two people in power by Facebook and other

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.220
platforms?

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.220
Right.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.710
That year ends with Occupy Wall Street.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.149
We think the people in power.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:04.070
So 2011, we really is the peak year of techno
democratic optimism.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.369
2012.

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:07.159
Barack Obama's reelected.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:10.069
Gay marriage resolutions are passing all around
the country.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:14.020
So it looks like this direction to history
is clearly one direction.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.380
And it would be the direction that would be
favored by by progressives.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:19.780
And then everything goes to hell.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.970
Beginning around 2014, a lot of weird stuff
happens in 2014.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:26.870
My argument in the essay is that tribalism
is very old.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.260
This left right dynamic is very old.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.750
But something changed in the 20 tens that
made us afraid of each other.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.500
That made it very easy for people to intimidate
each other, and at least to fragmentation

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.350
within the left, fragmentation within the
right.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.000
And the key metaphor.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.180
If you go back to the Tower of Babel story
in Genesis, it's a very short little story.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.980
The key line is that when God is offended
by humanity's hubris, he says, Let us go down

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:56.120
and confuse their language so that they may
not understand one another.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.320
And that's what's new.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.320
The Break.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.440
It's not just the cross party hatred that's
been going on for a while.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.440
We've had that before.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.320
If this new thing of just everything breaking
up, we can't understand each other.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:11.180
Speaker 1: That piece reminds me, you know,
oftentimes people will talk about Orwell's

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.810
1984.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:18.510
But what you just mentioned about the confusion
is really more reminiscent to me of Aldous

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:23.730
Huxley's Brave New World, where it's a sort
of, you could say, a fire hose of falsehood

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.980
or this inability to sort of discern what's
real or what isn't.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.880
Is that an apt analogy to what you're describing?

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.630
Speaker 5: You know, there are elements of
both, right?

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:36.130
We all read, you know, for my generation,
we all read those two works at the eighth

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.620
grade or whenever it was.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.420
And that's the way the 20th century thought
about things.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:45.070
Either it's going to be, you know, dictators
like Stalin and Hitler, or it's going to be

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:47.660
some like techno oppression.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:52.020
But neither of those anticipated the decentralization
revolution.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:57.550
That is what's happening now has some echoes
of, you know, the Stasi in East Germany.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:02.130
And people are afraid to speak and people
can rattle each other, but there's no dictator.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:06.760
And so just as you know, I've been learning
a little bit about cryptocurrencies and decentralized

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:07.760
finance.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.350
We talked about Defi in the same way you might
speak about De Tote, that is there going to

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:16.600
be decentralized totalitarianism without any
totalitarian or any leader?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.260
We can all be afraid of each other.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.500
And that has effects that suppress discourse,
suppress people dissenting on both sides.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.610
But moderates in particular are afraid to
speak up because on the right, you're going

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.350
to get death threats and be horrifically attacked.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.880
On the left, you'll be called a racist or
transphobe.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.840
So social media makes it very easy for the
passionate extremes to attack the moderates

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.290
and to attack leaders.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:41.070
That I think has messed us all up since around
2014.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:46.320
Speaker 1: How did Donald Trump sort of exploit
these circumstances?

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.900
Speaker 5: Donald Trump, the diagnosis I hear
from my friends who are clinical psychologist

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:56.220
is narcissistic personality disorder.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.510
He everything's about him.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.000
He is not an evil genius.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
He is not manic.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.590
And he's not like this like brilliant mannequin
and behind the scenes manipulator.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:04.660
He is what he is.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.720
He is a narcissist with very little self-control.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.960
And he just does what he does.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.070
And it just so happens that the political
world, the communications world went insane

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:20.570
around 2014 to allow someone like him to actually
be out there tweeting.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.780
And, you know, we have negative partizanship.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:28.210
The more you attack the other side, the more
people like you on your side.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.300
So my argument is that Donald Trump didn't
cause the collapse of the Tower of Babel.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.500
He certainly made things worse.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.480
He made everything worse, made the passions
worse, made the norms worse.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.400
I mean, I blame him for a lot, but had he
never run, most of these things would still

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.730
have happened.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.820
Not quite as intense, but they would still
happen.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:51.030
And he benefited because in the pre battle
era before we were all fragmented.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.360
People saying the things he said like that
was automatic exclusion.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.410
Like everybody would have shunned him know.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.150
But but afterwards nothing makes sense anymore.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:05:59.730
There is no overarching narrative.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.120
It's babble all the way forward.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:02.120
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:07.310
One of the things that I think resonates with
me in terms of understanding that period is

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:13.700
it seemed that it became much more acceptable
to publicly say a lot of the things that before

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.400
some people understood I think this, but I
probably shouldn't say it.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.720
I shouldn't say it at work, I shouldn't say
it on social media.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.370
It was sort of normalized to some degree.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:22.470
Speaker 5: That's right.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:23.470
Well, that's right.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:24.720
And there was such a thing as polite society.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.620
There were some norms that were widely shared,
maybe not on the far right and far left, but

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.560
there was a sort of a, you know, a general
consensus of polite society.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:33.940
And you could be outside.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.370
You could quickly get yourself outside polite
society.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:42.060
Part of what I'm arguing is that is that there's
always let's let let's see how much a voice

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.200
does do people have at each point on the spectrum?

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.350
And it's always the case that people who are
angry and loud are going to have more voice

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:49.350
because they try harder.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.030
So the middle has less and the extremes have
more.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:55.600
When social media comes in and I don't mean
early social media, I mean only after we get

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:58.560
the like button, the retweet button that's
2009.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.280
We get threaded comments in 2013 which encourages
everyone to fight with everyone in the comment

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:04.280
section.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:05.280
It's.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.620
This new Hyper-V realized environment.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:07.640
That's what changes things.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:13.340
And when that happens now, the extremes get
much more voiced and the middle 80% gets much

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:14.380
less.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:16.080
Many of us, we just keep our heads down.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:17.080
People are exhausted.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.080
They don't want to speak up.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.990
And we can't have a deliberative democracy
when most people don't want to even join the

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:23.990
discussion.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:27.270
And when the people in the discussion are
incentivized, literally incentivized to be

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.270
assholes.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:32.830
The more of an ask you are, the more you say
things that are extreme, the more you will

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.010
be successful.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.970
So the incentives are so antithetical to what
we need in our deliberative democracy.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.490
Speaker 1: You talked about the spectrum.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:45.100
There's this book, Waves of Rancor, which
I think might not even be in print anymore,

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:52.240
which talked about the early days of talk
radio, how it was dominated by sort of conservative

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.070
ownership, as well as by church radio, low
power church radio.

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:01.840
And that this was part of why the right started
to dominate talk radio now.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.780
One of the things I also think is a factor
is that these some of these platforms are

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.090
better vessels for some right wing ideas.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:16.521
So like, for example, if you think about taxes,
the right wing idea of taxes are bad, maybe

00:08:16.521 --> 00:08:18.570
they're theft, maybe they're slavery.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.389
Let's just make them as low as possible.

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:23.470
It's super simple and concise, and it's great
for talk radio.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.470
The position on the left is not Let's make
taxes as high as possible.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:30.699
It's well, we need the right level of taxation
to account for the social programs we write.

00:08:30.699 --> 00:08:33.389
So it's already sort of like structurally
a problem.

00:08:33.389 --> 00:08:40.159
Is this to some degree also a factor, but
maybe even more so when it comes to social

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:44.819
media, the number of characters that are allowed
on Twitter and this type of thing.

00:08:44.819 --> 00:08:46.639
Speaker 5: Absolutely.

00:08:46.639 --> 00:08:52.579
If you think about the conditions under which
people can have a discussion when they disagree,

00:08:52.579 --> 00:08:54.760
here's a really, really bad way to do it.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:59.670
How about let's bring people together and
have them be perhaps anonymous?

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:00.670
Let's bring people together.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.740
Have no shared past, no shared future.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.790
It's a temporary interaction.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:09.160
Let's bring them together in a place in which
you guarantee that there is no context.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:13.160
Whatever you say will be taken out of context
because there is no context, especially Twitter.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.110
Twitter, of course, being the worst of all.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.329
And let's do it.

00:09:17.329 --> 00:09:21.800
Let's have this discussion take place in the
middle of the Roman Coliseum so that the stands

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:25.149
or you're having a discussion with someone,
but you're not really talking to the person.

00:09:25.149 --> 00:09:26.790
You don't really care about the person.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:31.279
The audience is thousands of people all around
cheering for blood.

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:33.769
Some are on your side, some are on the other
person's side.

00:09:33.769 --> 00:09:36.390
Now, what are the odds that you can have a
good discussion about?

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:40.089
What do you think the proper tax rate should
be and what should we do about immigration?

00:09:40.089 --> 00:09:42.740
You know, you cannot have a discussion.

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:47.339
So so your question started with talk radio,
and that is a huge part of our polarization.

00:09:47.339 --> 00:09:50.740
That's something I didn't I mentioned in The
Atlantic article, I should have said spent

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:55.720
more time on talk radio, really does radicalize
the right in the beginning of seventies, but

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:56.990
especially in the nineties.

00:09:56.990 --> 00:10:03.319
Fox News and all the talk radio stars doesn't
have a similar effect on the left.

00:10:03.319 --> 00:10:09.079
But I think my sense is that while talk radio
made the right radicalize in the nineties,

00:10:09.079 --> 00:10:12.060
social media made the left radicalize in the
mid 20 tens.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:16.389
There's been a lot written from that Iglesias,
Origen and others calling it the Great Awakening.

00:10:16.389 --> 00:10:21.910
An intensification of wokeness is the word
used by the right, but it really did change

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:22.990
the dynamics on the left.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:27.480
There's always been and there still is in
the Democratic Party, a healthy debate between

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.420
where the far left and the moderate left.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:34.360
So in the party, that dynamic is still going
on, whereas in the Republican Party there

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:35.360
is none.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.779
All the moderates are dead or expelled.

00:10:36.779 --> 00:10:41.130
So the Republican Party, I say in the paper,
the Republican Party has become the stupid

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:42.130
party.

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:43.480
You become stupid when you shoot your moderates.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.209
If you don't have dissent, if you have orthodoxy,
you become stupid.

00:10:47.209 --> 00:10:48.209
You'll be wrong on most things.

00:10:48.209 --> 00:10:49.929
The Republican Party is the stupid party.

00:10:49.929 --> 00:10:51.439
The Democrats are not.

00:10:51.439 --> 00:10:57.000
But my argument is that if you look at many
of our epistemic institutions, that is those

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.850
that generate knowledge universities, media,
the arts, journalism.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:03.149
These, of course, are dominated by progressives.

00:11:03.149 --> 00:11:07.089
That's always been the case, and it's very
much the case now when those places like The

00:11:07.089 --> 00:11:11.699
New York Times, when people can't dissent
within a progressive organization, then that

00:11:11.699 --> 00:11:14.420
organization becomes structurally stupid.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:19.300
And so I think social media has intensified
the power of progressive activists in a way

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:21.249
that has often suppressed dissent on the left.

00:11:21.249 --> 00:11:24.350
And I think that actually, in the long run,
I think that hurts the left.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:29.350
Speaker 1: Yeah, we talked before the start
of the interview about how there is an asymmetry

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:34.009
in how the extremes on either side are sort
of impacted by the status quo.

00:11:34.009 --> 00:11:38.660
I'd love to hear you talk a little bit about
that, but I also think it's important to mention

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:40.821
the extreme of the left, however we define
it.

00:11:40.821 --> 00:11:45.179
And I think sometimes what's called the extreme
maybe isn't that extreme by global standards,

00:11:45.179 --> 00:11:46.990
but let's put that aside for a moment.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:52.389
It doesn't seem to have that much power in
the Democratic Party, as evidenced by on Reddit

00:11:52.389 --> 00:11:54.230
and Twitter in 2020.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:58.689
It looked like Bernie was just running away
with this thing and it wasn't even close in

00:11:58.689 --> 00:12:02.119
terms of how Democratic voters actually voted
in the primaries.

00:12:02.119 --> 00:12:03.850
Speaker 5: That's right.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:04.850
No, you're right.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:05.950
It doesn't have that much power within the
party.

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:07.879
And that's the asymmetry.

00:12:07.879 --> 00:12:11.889
There's there's a great quote from John Stuart
Mill that something like in all of the great

00:12:11.889 --> 00:12:16.829
disputes about social matters, each side has
been right in what it affirmed the wrong and

00:12:16.829 --> 00:12:17.829
what it denied.

00:12:17.829 --> 00:12:23.019
And when I talk to people on the left, they
can I mean, the number of crimes committed

00:12:23.019 --> 00:12:27.360
by the Republican Party, certainly from denying
Barack Obama's Supreme Court appointment to

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:29.250
trying to storm the Capitol to overturn.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:33.769
I mean, the crimes are it's unbelievable the
crimes the Republican Party has committed.

00:12:33.769 --> 00:12:37.019
And there is no comparison to what the Democrats
have done.

00:12:37.019 --> 00:12:38.579
So people who left are certain of that.

00:12:38.579 --> 00:12:39.579
And they're right.

00:12:39.579 --> 00:12:40.970
They're absolutely right.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:43.329
Why don't those damn Republicans realize that?

00:12:43.329 --> 00:12:45.269
Well, now talk to conservatives there.

00:12:45.269 --> 00:12:47.379
You know, some will acknowledge that.

00:12:47.379 --> 00:12:51.350
Yeah, you know, the party, you know, I'm not
so comfortable with this, but and then, boy,

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:55.959
do they have a list of crimes of what the
CBC did, what the media did, what the universities

00:12:55.959 --> 00:12:56.959
did.

00:12:56.959 --> 00:12:59.959
And they've got videos and they've got quotes
